    id: "econ-2021-ol-q15-a-i", topicId: "economics-4-2", conceptId: "effects-of-brexit-on-consumers-and-firms",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2021 OL Q16(c)(i)",
    stem: "On 1 January 2020, the UK officially left the EU. This has become known as Brexit.",
    questionText: "Discuss one economic effect Brexit will have on each of the following:",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"2 @ 8","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 16,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-consumers", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "An effect on consumers in Ireland — any one", marks: 8, contextNote: "One effect on consumers, 8 marks (4 + 4).", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":8,"options":["Increased prices: VAT and customs charges may apply on the delivery of goods to the Republic from the UK and so goods imported from the UK will costs more.","Decreased standard of living: the increases in the price of goods/services means that consumers will have less disposable income. This leads to a lower standard of living.","Encourage import substitution: the increase in the price of goods/services from the UK may encourage consumers in Ireland to ‘shop local’ and substitute Irish produced goods rather than goods from in the UK.","Consumer rights (Not in EU): purchases from EU-based websites come with various consumer rights, including the right to return goods for up to 14 days and various dispute resolution mechanisms, but these may no longer apply to online retailers in the UK.","Possible shortages of goods: one of the effects of Brexit was problems in supply chains resulting in the shortage of some goods on supermarket shelves."]} },
    { id: "r-businesses", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "An effect on businesses in Ireland — any one", marks: 8, contextNote: "One effect on businesses, 8 marks (4 + 4). The scheme prices the pair as one 2 @ 8.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":1,"perOption":8,"options":["Reduced sales / profits: Irish firms faced with possible tariffs may face reduced sales and profits.","Possible closure / new markets: If Irish firms can’t find alternative buyers it may force the closure of some businesses in Ireland. Some mushroom producers in Ireland have ceased trading.","Possible relocation to UK: some Irish exporters who rely on trade to the UK may decide to relocate their business to the UK to avoid tariffs. This could increase unemployment and Irish exports.","Problems with supply chains: some Irish firms have experienced difficulties with the supply of raw materials / goods due to difficulties at UK ports."]} },
    ],
