    year: 2022, level: "ordinary",
    paperFileid: "LC034GLP000EV",
    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2022 Ordinary Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2022-ol-sa-q8-i", topicId: "economics-2-2", conceptId: "vaccination-externality-and-opportunity-cost",
    section: "A", questionRef: "2022 OL Section A Q8",
    stem: "51.6% of the world population has received at least one dose of a COVID-19 vaccine. This situation has led to positive externalities. (An externality refers to the external costs (or external benefits) that accrue to other individuals or to society as a result of production or consumption.)",
    questionText: "State one example of a positive externality in relation to vaccinations. Outline one opportunity cost for the Irish government of providing the COVID-19 vaccine for their citizens.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal","notation":"15","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 15,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-externality", kind: "point", verbatim: "Protection of others from contracting the virus: their health is protected, and they can continue to attend work school and college / reduces the spread of the virus.\n\nReduced pressure on health services: EDs will require less beds to deal with C19 patients and the EDs can operate more efficiently.\n\nAllows society function: allows people to start resuming life as restrictions are lifted or lightened.\n\nCommercial activity can resume: businesses can reopen with a positive effect on employment and government finances.\n\nReduce the need for government interference in how we live, allowing more freedom and thus the government can concentrate on its core activity. With greater protection in the population the long -term cost to the government is reduced.\n\nHealthier population with less days off work meaning workers have higher disposable income than if they had been on PUP etc.", marks: null, contextNote: "One positive externality of vaccination. Complete BOTH parts. The scheme prices (i) and (ii) together as one 15: the first correct answer 9 marks (6 + 3) and the second 6 (3 + 3), with no fixed share for either part.", openList: true },
    { id: "r-opportunity-cost", kind: "point", verbatim: "The money the government could have used to provide other services for citizens such as childcare subsidy, respite for Carers, improved pensions etc.", marks: null, contextNote: "One opportunity cost for the government. Name what else the money could have paid for.", openList: true },
    ],
